<article class="env-lemma" id="063M">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063M">Lemma <span data-tag="063M">31.22.5</span>.</a> Let $i : Z \to X$ be an immersion of schemes. Then $i$ is a quasi-regular immersion if and only if the following conditions are satisfied </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$i$ is locally of finite presentation, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the conormal sheaf $\mathcal{C}_{Z/X}$ is finite locally free, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the map (<a href="/tag/0632" data-tag="0632">31.20.1.2</a>) is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      An open immersion is locally of finite presentation. Hence we may replace $X$ by an open subscheme $U \subset X$ such that $i$ identifies $Z$ with a closed subscheme of $U$, i.e., we may assume that $i$ is a closed immersion. Let $\mathcal{I} \subset \mathcal{O}_ X$ be the corresponding quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals. Recall, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01TV" data-tag="01TV">29.22.7</a> that $\mathcal{I}$ is of finite type if and only if $i$ is locally of finite presentation. Hence the equivalence follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/063H" data-tag="063H">31.21.4</a> and unwinding the definitions. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>